.TH BIGGUNS 1
.SH NAME
bigguns \- artillery game
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B temple/bigguns
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I BigGuns
is a port of the TempleOS
.B Demo/Games/BigGuns.HC
artillery game.
It runs under
.IR wm (1).
.PP
Aim a barrel on a scrolling height-map, fire shells under gravity, and
pan the camera with comma and full stop.  Craters are cut into the
height-map, so the ground a later shell lands on is the ground the
earlier ones left.  The map is 2048 pixels wide and there are 512 dust
motes blowing across it on the wind.
.PP
A shell hits when its own circle touches the ground, not when its centre
passes a number.  The original asks that question by drawing the circle
into the map in
.BR ROP_COLLISION ,
a raster operation that paints nothing and counts the pixels it would
have set that are not the sky colour; since the map holds nothing but the
terrain, that reduces exactly to asking whether any point of a radius-2
circle is at or below the elevation, which is what is done here.
.PP
Two panels open it, both the original's: Terry Davis's \fI"I refuse to
rip-off the original, so this is intentionally crappy"\fP, and then
\fI"The map scrolls."\fP  Any key dismisses each.
.PP
The controls are:
.TF space
.TP
.B Left / a
Aim barrel left
.TP
.B Right / d
Aim barrel right
.TP
.B space
Fire shell
.TP
.B ,
Pan camera left
.TP
.B .
Pan camera right
.TP
.B Enter
Restart
.TP
.B Esc / q
Quit
.PD
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/temple/bigguns.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR temple (1),
.IR draw3d (2),
.IR ode (2),
.IR tone (2),
.IR wm (1)
.SH BUGS
The gun barrel and its base are placed by the sprite's own origin and
turned about it, which is what
.I Sprite3
and
.I Sprite3ZB
do; the barrel's origin is eighteen pixels from the middle of its
bitmap, so placing the bitmap instead put the gun visibly out.
The mirror the original applies while aiming left is a symmetry plane
through the gun, expressed here as a flipped sprite turned the other way
\- reflecting a rotation about a vertical axis is the same as reflecting
the source and turning the other way.
That identity is exact at two of the four angles it was checked at and
differs by a pixel's worth at the others, which is where the resampler
lands rather than where the geometry says.
